From: "D.R.racer" <drracer@seznam.cz>
Date: Sun, 18 Oct 2026 13:31:34 +0200
Subject: drm/sun4i: tv: add VGA output for A10/A20

The A10/A20 TV encoders can drive a VGA connector: in "VGA mode" three
DACs output R, G and B taken straight from the display backend, and
the TCON channel 1 provides HSYNC and VSYNC. The Cubietruck has its
VGA connector wired this way, and the vendor 3.4 kernel uses it as its
default output.

When the TV encoder's output port is linked to a "vga-connector" node,
register a DAC encoder and a VGA connector instead of the composite
one. The connector reads EDID and detects the monitor over the DDC bus
given by the connector's ddc-i2c-bus property, and falls back to the
standard modes up to 1920x1200 when the monitor gives no EDID; an EDID
override still applies first. Without
a DDC bus it leaves the modes to the probe helper, which applies an
EDID override when the connector is forced on and otherwise adds the
modes up to 1024x768. The I2C adapter is looked up when first
needed rather than at probe or bind time, so the display pipeline
never waits for an I2C controller.

The register values are those of the vendor driver (TVE_set_vga_mode),
checked against a Cubietruck running it. The backend keeps its RGB
output (no RGB to YUV conversion as for composite). Modes are limited
to 150 MHz, which covers the vendor driver's VGA modes up to 1680x1050
(146.25 MHz); faster modes are untested.

VGA works with the TV encoder's reset bit untouched, as with the vendor
driver: on a Cubietruck driving VGA the bit (CCU LCD0 CH0 bit 29) reads
0, i.e. asserted. So the reset line is optional for the VGA case;
composite still requires it.

Also stop the graph walk at VGA connectors: sun4i_drv would otherwise
add the connector node as a component that never binds.

Assisted-by: Claude:claude-opus-5-5
